<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ambaris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 713 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ambaridi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ambaris was a king of Bit-Burutash in Tabal in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the late 8th century BCE. The Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> gave him his daughter in marriage and the land of Hilakku, but in 713 BCE accused him of plotting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> and Rusa of Urartu, deported him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and annexed his kingdom as a province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ambaris.jpg" alt="Ambaris" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans van Deukeren (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NeoHittiteStates.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
